        "description": "<p>We're a small business that sells pre-booked appointments with homeowners to local contractors, billed per appointment that actually happens.<p>Our first client agreed to this on a recorded call and saved his card through a $1 Stripe payment link, which we planned to use for billing later. That $1 payment went through.<p>A few weeks later we invoiced him $1,500 for 3 completed appointments and charged the saved card. Stripe blocked the payment. Two days after that the account was closed with this wording: payments \"don't appear to have been authorized by the customer.\"<p>What happened next:<p>- We appealed through the Dashboard with an appeal letter, our company documents and the invoice. It was rejected the next day as an \"unacceptable level of risk.\"<p>- Support told us our \"business model presents a level of risk we're unable to support\" and that the decision is final.<p>- After a Stripe employee escalated it internally, we got a short email saying Stripe still won't accept paym",
